            <ArticleTitle>Force of limb withdrawals elicited by graded noxious heat compared with other behavioral measures of carrageenan-induced hyperalgesia and allodynia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>139-49</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We investigated if carrageenan-induced thermal hyperalgesia and mechanical allodynia are associated with a reduction in threshold and-or enhancement of suprathreshold nocifensive responses, using a method to measure the force of a hind limb wilhdrawal reflex elicited by graded noxious heat stimuli (36-52 degrees C, 5s) delivered by Peltier thermode tethered to the ventral hind paw of conscious rats. Withdrawal reflexes were recorded 2.5 h after intraplantar injection of carrageenan (1 or 0.1%) or saline vehicle in sessions &gt;2 weeks apart: baseline reflexes were assessed the day before. Withdrawal reflex force increased linearly from 42-52 degrees C. Carrageenan 1% significantly enhanced withdrawal reflexes at 40-46 degrees C, reducing the slope and threshold of the stimulus-response function. This was associated with significant reductions in thermal paw withdrawal latency (Hargreaves test: by 50%), mechanical withdrawal threshold (by 82%) and weight bearing on the injected side (by 81%) measured with independent force plates. Smaller reductions in thermal paw withdrawal latency and mechanical withdrawal threshold, and smaller enhancement of withdrawal reflex force, were observed following 0.1% carrageenan. Intraplantar saline was ineffective. This method allows assessment of hyperalgesic changes in stimulus-response coding over a broad range of noxious stimulus intensities.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
